Conclusion

This laboratory demonstrates how a seemingly protected application can remain vulnerable to client-side prototype pollution when its sanitization mechanism relies on an incomplete blacklist.

The standard vectors:

__proto__
constructor
prototype

were blocked successfully.

However, the alternative payload:

/?__pro__proto__to__[foo]=bar

was able to bypass the sanitization logic because the dangerous property name was reconstructed after the filtering process.

After confirming prototype pollution, the transport_url property was identified as a gadget because it controls the src attribute of a dynamically created <script> element.

The final payload:

/?__pro__proto__to__[transport_url]=data:,alert(1);

successfully chained the prototype pollution source with the script.src gadget and resulted in JavaScript execution.

The complete attack can therefore be summarized as:

Blacklist Bypass
       +
Prototype Pollution
       +
Inherited Property
       +
script.src Gadget
